#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kb-due — which knowledge trees are due for a hygiene sweep, and (with --nudge) everything
else a librarian should act on.

A cadence nobody is reminded of is a preference, not a cadence. The sweep itself
(`kb-audit`) needs an agent and cannot be automated, so the machinery is only this:
record when each tree was last swept, work out when it is due, and say so where an
agent is already standing — the session-start hook calls this.

WHAT IT READS, from each tree's INDEX.md:
  last_swept: YYYY-MM-DD    written by kb-audit when it finishes a sweep. It lives
                            here and not in needs-attention.md because a CLEAN sweep
                            writes no queue file at all, and would erase the only
                            evidence it had run.
  sweep_interval: <N>d      how long a sweep stays good for. Explicit, not derived
                            from freshness_horizon: a 90d horizon would imply a 9d
                            nag and a 14d project a 1.4d one, surprising in both
                            directions. Default when absent: 30d.

SILENCE IS THE POINT. Nothing due prints nothing (exit 0) in --quiet or --nudge (and, in --nudge, every
sourced section asserted, #239): a
reminder that fires every session is ignored by the time it matters.

--NUDGE (Phase 5): the owner's cadence decision for kb-audit AND kb-distill is "surfaced
when due, never run unattended" — one nudge, not two. --quiet's sweep-only report is kept
exactly as it was (every current caller of --quiet is unaffected); --nudge is additive and
reports, per tree, everything a librarian should act on:

Each tree's line leads with its assertion coverage (#239, ADR-0022): "assertion coverage 2 of 5 sourced
sections", asserted sections over sourced ones, a lint-flagged assertion counted as unasserted. Zero
is stated as 0 of N, never a warning. Coverage is a measure, not something due, but a tree below full
coverage is named even when nothing is due; only a tree with nothing due and every sourced section
asserted (or none sourced) prints nothing.

  1. sweep due            — --quiet's own check, unchanged. Left out of the nudge while a pending kb-audit
                             packet covers it (the packet is already named), and the notes in 4 while a
                             pending kb-distill packet does (#204), as kb-report does.
  2. facts due for recheck — reuses kb-verify's own staleness selection (its horizon/short-lease
                             test, kbengine.lease), through kbengine.attention — one judgment of
                             "is this section due", not two copies of it that can drift apart. The count
                             is every due assertion; when it is more than the verifier_budget, ", N a run" says how
                             many one run takes (#211). Only a count and the command
                             (`kb-verify <path> --yes`) are printed; no assertion is ever run or even read here.
  3. open queue items      — `status: open` records in the library's needs-attention.md, read
                             through kbengine.queue (#202)
                             so a third copy of "what a queue record looks like" never exists.
  4. episodic notes awaiting distillation — a note kb-distill has distilled carries a heading
                             beginning "## Distill" (kb-distill Step 5 now names "## Distilled";
                             older notes say "## Distillation record", which the same prefix
                             matches). A note without one is counted.
  5. unstamped sources     — Source: lines with no Verified: stamp of their own, in sections that
                             are not CONFLICTED, read with check-kb's own section reader (the one
                             its verified: floor uses). Counted per source, since §6 stacks one
                             stamp per source (issue #64). §7 ranks unknown ahead of stale, and
                             until now only a sweep surfaced them (issue #29). Once a tree has
                             been swept, only the ones new since the sweep's commit are named
                             (issue #142): the commit that last changed last_swept in INDEX.md, read
                             from git. Never swept, or not in git: the full count. A sweep not yet
                             committed: nothing is new. Plain kb-due (no flag) shows the full count.
     unsourced sections   — sections with content and no Source: line, in fact files, read with
                             check-kb's own reader (§6, issue #154). Shown beside unstamped sources:
                             nothing was cited, so there is nothing to recheck.
  6. stale, no assertion   — stamped sections past the horizon or short lease that carry no
                             Recheck:, by kbengine.lease's own judgement. kb-verify cannot settle them,
                             so they are told apart from category 2, and the budget does not cap
                             them (issue #63).

  A tree with nothing due in any of the six contributes no line at all, and so does a tree whose
  root INDEX.md declares `archived: <date or reason>` (issue #141): a read-only archive cannot act on
  a nudge. Plain kb-due still lists it, marked archived. A registered library is served whatever it
  declares, so it is nudged as usual and told to use kb-library remove. A library registered
  read-only (ADR-0020) is a consumer's copy, curated by its publisher: it drops out too, and plain
  kb-due lists it as read-only. A failure computing one
  category for one tree (a malformed needs-attention.md, an INDEX.md with no freshness_horizon) is
  printed as a one-line warning to stderr and that category is skipped for that tree only — every
  other tree and every other category still gets computed. This hook must never crash a session.

--SUMMARY (with --nudge, issue #79): also print, as the LAST line, the same nudge condensed to one
line beginning "KB due — ", for the session-start hook to show the person (a systemMessage),
while the full nudge above it goes to the model. One run gives both, so the two cannot disagree.

--SESSION (the session-start hook): decide from kb.nudge (the registry's, or $KB_NUDGE over it: always, weekly:<day>
or off) and today whether to show the nudge, before computing anything. Stdout is then what the hook adds to the
model's context: the setting's note or warning, and the nudge under a heading. It is the schedule's one home (#253).

--SUMMARY-OUT FILE writes what the person is shown to FILE: the summary line, after the warning when the setting
is one this does not understand. Empty when there is nothing to show. The nudge body still goes to stdout, so the
caller needs no text matching.

Usage: kb-due [tree ...] [--quiet|--nudge [--summary] [--summary-out FILE] [--session]]
  Scans every library registered on this machine (ADR-0014; kb-library list) plus any further
  trees given (a project knowledge/ tree).
  --quiet   print only overdue trees, and nothing at all when none are (sweep only)
  --nudge   print the full librarian nudge (review packets + sweep + recheck + queue + episodic + unstamped + unsourced + stale), and
            nothing at all when nothing in any category is due
Exit 0 always: being due is a nudge, never a failure.
"""
import importlib.machinery, importlib.util, pathlib, shlex, subprocess, sys
from typing import NamedTuple

from kbengine import attention, clock, contract, env, registry, reports
from kbengine.attention import Failed, describe_coverage, label


# -------------------------------------------------------------------- --nudge ----
def load_module(name: str, filename: str):
    """Import an extensionless sibling script as a module, byte-identical to the file on
    disk — not a copy. No .py suffix means spec_from_file_location can't infer a loader from
    the extension, so an explicit SourceFileLoader is required, and bytecode caching is turned
    off first so an engine script that only ever reads these trees doesn't litter scripts/__pycache__."""
    path = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent / filename
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name, str(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


def plural(n: int, word: str) -> str:
    return f"{n} {word}" if n == 1 else f"{n} {word}s"


# The --summary categories, in the order they are shown: (key, heading).
SUMMARY = (("report", "weekly report"), ("packets", "to decide"), ("sweep", "sweeps due"), ("recheck", "to recheck"), ("stale", "stale, no assertion"),
           ("queue", "queue"), ("queue_unreadable", "unreadable queue"), ("distill", "to distill"), ("unstamped", "unstamped"), ("unsourced", "unsourced"),
           ("contract", "contract"))   # all but the report are item kinds


def summary_line(counts: list) -> str:
    """One line for the person from [(label, {key: count-or-text})]: each category with the
    trees in it, at most three named. Empty when nothing is due."""
    out = []
    for key, heading in SUMMARY:
        items = [f"{lbl} {c[key]}".rstrip() for lbl, c in counts if c.get(key)]
        if items:
            more = f", +{len(items) - 3} more" if len(items) > 3 else ""
            out.append(f"{heading}: {', '.join(items[:3])}{more}")
    return "KB due — " + " · ".join(out) if out else ""


# What it says when a kind of item could not be worked out: warned to stderr and skipped, never a stop.
# Not said: the records the queue reader skipped and the stubs marked manual, which are not worth a warning.
FAILED = {"recheck": "could not compute facts due for recheck", "stale": "could not compute stale facts with no assertion",
          "queue": "could not read needs-attention.md", "distill": "could not count episodic notes",
          "unstamped": "could not count unstamped sections", "unsourced": "could not count unsourced sections",
          "contract": "could not read the contract version", "coverage": "could not work out assertion coverage"}


def contract_text(c) -> str:
    """The contract item in a line: what is wrong with it, the same for every state that needs telling."""
    st, v, newest, _newer = c
    if st == "behind":
        return (f"built to contract {contract.fmt(v)}; this engine knows {contract.fmt(newest)} "
                f"(CONVENTIONS §14 says what it adds; check-kb lists it)")
    if st == "ahead":
        return (f"declares contract {contract.fmt(v)}, newer than this engine "
                f"({contract.fmt(newest)}) — upgrade the engine")
    if st == "unsupported":
        return f"declares contract {contract.fmt(v)}, which this engine does not support — not served"
    return "kb_contract cannot be read — not served (check-kb says why)"


def word(item, att, retire):
    """The nudge's words for one item that says something: terse, in the nudge's voice."""
    n, d = item.n, item.detail
    if item.kind == "packets":
        return f"{plural(n, 'review packet')} awaiting your decision (kb-apply list)"
    if item.kind == "sweep":
        when = "never swept" if d.age is None else f"last swept {d.age}d ago"
        return f"sweep due ({when}, every {d.interval}d)"
    if item.kind == "recheck":
        # The whole backlog, not the budget's worth: one run takes `budget` of them (#211).
        per_run = f", {d.budget} a run" if n > d.budget else ""
        return f"{plural(n, 'fact')} due for recheck{per_run} (kb-verify {shlex.quote(str(att.root))} --yes)"
    if item.kind == "stale":
        return f"{plural(n, 'stale fact')} with no assertion (re-check by hand, or kb-audit)"
    if item.kind == "queue":
        cats = {}
        for r in d:
            cats[r.get("category", "?")] = cats.get(r.get("category", "?"), 0) + 1
        by_cat = ", ".join(f"{v} {k}" for k, v in sorted(cats.items()))
        return f"{plural(n, 'open queue item')} ({by_cat})"
    if item.kind == "queue_unreadable":   # records the parser skips are not counted above, so they are named here (#168)
        return f"{plural(n, 'unreadable queue record')} (check-kb names the lines)"
    if item.kind == "distill":
        return f"{plural(n, 'episodic note')} awaiting distillation"
    if item.kind == "unstamped":
        if not d.since_sweep:
            return f"{plural(n, 'unstamped source')} (never verified)"
        return f"{plural(n, 'new unstamped source')} since the last sweep (never verified)"
    if item.kind == "unsourced":
        return f"{plural(n, 'unsourced section')} (no Source: line)"
    if item.kind == "contract":
        return contract_text(d)
    if item.kind == "archived":
        return (f"declares archived: {d}, but is registered and still served "
                f"(kb-library remove {retire} to retire it)")


def summary_counts(items) -> dict:
    """{summary key: count-or-text} for the --summary line, from the same items the nudge words. The
    summary keys are the items' kinds."""
    keys = {key for key, _ in SUMMARY}
    out = {}
    for item in items:
        if item.kind not in keys or not item.says:
            continue
        if item.kind == "sweep":
            out["sweep"] = " "     # named, not counted: one sweep per tree
        elif item.kind == "contract":
            c = item.detail
            out["contract"] = "(unreadable)" if c.state == "malformed" else f"{contract.fmt(c.version)} ({c.state})"
        else:
            out[item.kind] = item.n
    return out


def nudge_for(att, retire=None):
    """(line-or-None, [warning, ...], {summary key: count}) for one tree's Attention, worded from its
    items (the policy of what needs saying, and when, is attention.items). A field that failed is a
    warning, never a stop to the other categories, this tree's own line, or any other tree — see module
    docstring: a malformed or uncomputable category is reported once and skipped."""
    lbl = label(att.tree)
    items = att.items
    warnings, coverage = [], next(i for i in items if i.kind == "coverage")
    for item in items:
        if item is coverage:
            continue   # leads the line, but is judged last
        if item.failed:
            if item.kind in FAILED:
                warnings.append(f"{lbl}: {FAILED[item.kind]} ({item.detail.reason}) — skipped")
        elif item.kind == "unstamped" and item.detail.git_error is not None:
            warnings.append(f"{lbl}: could not read the last sweep from git ({item.detail.git_error}) — full count shown")
    parts = [word(item, att, retire) for item in attention.nudgeable(items)]
    if coverage.failed:   # ADR-0022: where trust is thin leads the line, before what is due (#239)
        warnings.append(f"{lbl}: {FAILED['coverage']} ({coverage.detail.reason}) — skipped")
    elif attention.worth_a_line(items):   # below full coverage is named even when nothing is due
        parts.insert(0, describe_coverage(coverage.detail))
    line = f"{lbl}: " + "; ".join(parts) if parts else None
    return line, warnings, summary_counts(items)


# ------------------------------------------------------------- when the nudge is shown ----
# The person's choice (issue #174): kb.nudge in the registry, or $KB_NUDGE over it. always (default) |
# weekly:<day> | off. It gates only the session-start hook (--session): `kb-due --nudge` on demand,
# kb-schedule and kb-report are unaffected, and the library-health messages the hook prints are never
# gated, because they change what retrieval can serve. $KB_TODAY stands in for today's date.
DAYS = {"mon": "mon", "monday": "mon", "tue": "tue", "tues": "tue", "tuesday": "tue", "wed": "wed", "wednesday": "wed",
        "thu": "thu", "thur": "thu", "thurs": "thu", "thursday": "thu", "fri": "fri", "friday": "fri",
        "sat": "sat", "saturday": "sat", "sun": "sun", "sunday": "sun"}
WEEKDAYS = ("mon", "tue", "wed", "thu", "fri", "sat", "sun")
ON_DEMAND = "Do not start upkeep unprompted; kb-due --nudge reports what is due on demand."


class Schedule(NamedTuple):
    show: bool       # show the nudge this session
    note: str        # said to the model instead of the nudge, when it is not shown
    warning: str     # the setting is not one this understands: said to the model and the person, and the nudge is shown


def nudge_setting() -> str:
    """kb.nudge: $KB_NUDGE, else the registry's, lower-cased with its spaces removed; `always` when unset."""
    raw = env.nudge_override()
    if not raw:
        registry_file = env.registry_path()
        if registry_file.is_file():
            r = subprocess.run(["git", "config", "--file", str(registry_file), "--get", "kb.nudge"], capture_output=True, text=True)
            raw = r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""
    return "".join((raw or "").lower().split()) or "always"


def schedule(setting: str, today, latest=None) -> Schedule:
    """Whether to show the nudge on `today` (a date) under `setting` (nudge_setting()'s normal form).
    `latest` is the newest report's path, named when the nudge is held back for the week."""
    if setting == "always":
        return Schedule(True, "", "")
    if setting == "off":
        return Schedule(False, f"The librarian's nudge is off on this machine (kb.nudge=off). {ON_DEMAND}", "")
    if setting.startswith("weekly:"):
        want = DAYS.get(setting[len("weekly:"):])
        if want is None:
            return Schedule(True, "", f"kb.nudge '{setting}' is not a weekday (use weekly:mon .. weekly:sun) — showing the nudge every session.")
        if WEEKDAYS[today.weekday()] != want:
            return Schedule(False, f"The librarian's nudge is weekly (kb.nudge=weekly:{want}) and is not shown today. {ON_DEMAND}"
                                   + (f" Latest kb-report: {latest}" if latest else ""), "")
        return Schedule(True, "", "")
    return Schedule(True, "", f"kb.nudge '{setting}' is not one of always, weekly:<day>, off — showing the nudge every session.")


HEADER = "Due for a librarian's attention — nothing printed here means nothing is due and every sourced section is asserted:"


def session_block(sched: Schedule, body) -> str:
    """What the session-start hook adds to the model's context: the setting's note or warning, then the nudge."""
    out = f"\nWARNING: {sched.warning}" if sched.warning else (f"\n{sched.note}" if sched.note else "")
    return out + (f"\n{HEADER}\n{body}" if sched.show and body else "")


def main(argv) -> int:
    nudge = "--nudge" in argv
    quiet = "--quiet" in argv
    summary = "--summary" in argv
    session = "--session" in argv
    summary_out = None
    if "--summary-out" in argv:
        i = argv.index("--summary-out")
        if i + 1 >= len(argv):
            print("kb-due: --summary-out needs a file", file=sys.stderr); return 2
        summary_out = pathlib.Path(argv[i + 1])
        argv = argv[:i] + argv[i + 2:]
    args = [a for a in argv if a not in ("--quiet", "--nudge", "--summary", "--session")]

    sched = Schedule(True, "", "")
    if session:   # the session-start hook: the person's kb.nudge decides, before anything is computed
        sched = schedule(nudge_setting(), clock.today(), reports.newest())
        if not sched.show:
            if summary_out:
                summary_out.write_text("")
            print(session_block(sched, None))
            return 0

    trees, registered, consumed = [], {}, set()
    try:   # the registry's one reader; an unreadable registry must not crash a session
        registered = {p: n for n, p in registry.installed().items()}
        consumed = registry.read_only_paths()   # ADR-0020
        trees += list(registered)
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"kb-due: library registry unreadable, registered libraries skipped: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    trees += [pathlib.Path(a) for a in args]

    packets = []
    if nudge:
        try:
            packets = load_module("kb_apply", "kb-apply").pending()
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"kb-due: review packets unreadable, skipped: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    rows = [attention.attention(t, packets, consumed, set(registered)) for t in trees if (t / "INDEX.md").is_file()]
    # An archived tree that is not registered drops out of the nudge and of --quiet (issue #141).
    # A read-only library is a consumer's copy, curated by its publisher (ADR-0020): it too drops out.
    shelved = attention.shelved
    due = [a for a in rows if a.sweep.due and not shelved(a)]

    if nudge:
        lines, warnings, counts = [], [], []
        for a in rows:
            if shelved(a):
                continue
            retire = registered.get(a.root) if a.archived else None
            line, warn, c = nudge_for(a, retire)
            if line:
                lines.append(line)
            warnings += warn
            counts.append((label(a.tree), c))
        try:   # the weekly report (ADR-0017): named while it is this week's and lists decisions
            rep = reports.current()
        except Exception as e:
            warnings.append(f"the weekly report is unreadable ({e}) — skipped")
            rep = None
        if rep:
            lines.append(f"Weekly report: {rep.decisions} decision(s) waiting in {rep.path}")
            counts.append(("report", {"report": rep.decisions}))
        for w in warnings:
            print(f"kb-due: {w}", file=sys.stderr)
        body = None
        if lines:
            body = ("\n".join(lines) + "\nCommands: kb-audit <library> for a sweep; kb-verify <path> --yes for "
                    "the rechecks above; kb-distill for the episodic notes above; kb-audit "
                    "<library> queues unstamped sources and stale facts with no assertion.")
        line = summary_line(counts) if lines else ""
        if summary_out:   # what the person is shown: the setting's warning, then the summary
            summary_out.write_text(" · ".join(x for x in (sched.warning, line) if x))
        if session:
            if sched.warning or body:
                print(session_block(sched, body))
        elif body:
            print(body)
            if summary:
                print(line)
        return 0

    if quiet:
        for a in due:
            when = "never swept" if a.sweep.age is None else f"last swept {a.sweep.age}d ago"
            print(f"{label(a.tree)}: {when} (every {a.sweep.interval}d) — kb-audit due")
        return 0

    if not rows:
        print("kb-due: no knowledge trees found"); return 0
    for a in rows:
        when = "never swept" if a.sweep.age is None else f"{a.sweep.age}d ago"
        if a.read_only:
            print(f"  ro   {label(a.tree):<28} read-only: curated by its publisher, not here")
            continue
        if shelved(a):
            print(f"  arch {label(a.tree):<28} archived: {a.archived}")
            continue
        un = a.unstamped_total   # the full count, never the nudge's new-since-sweep one (#142)
        un = 0 if isinstance(un, Failed) else un
        print(f"  {'DUE ' if a.sweep.due else 'ok  '} {label(a.tree):<28} swept {when:<14} "
              f"(every {a.sweep.interval}d)" + (f"  {plural(un, 'unstamped source')}" if un else ""))
    print(f"\n{len(due)} of {len(rows)} tree(s) due for a sweep"
          + ("" if due else " — nothing to do"))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(clock.guard(main, sys.argv[1:]))
